Docker Engine is provided by ThothDock
======================================

This system runs inside ThothTerm. The container engine is ThothDock, a
userspace Docker Engine API implementation running beside the guest, reachable
at unix:///run/thothdock/thothdock.sock. The Docker CLI talks to it.

The stock engine packages (docker.io, docker-ce, docker-engine, moby-engine,
containerd, containerd.io, runc) are replaced by empty placeholders versioned
9999:1.0+thothdock.N, pinned at priority 1001, so "apt upgrade" and
"apt full-upgrade" leave them alone and "apt install docker.io" reports them
as already installed. Installing a real one at another version is refused by
/usr/lib/thothdock/engine-guard-hook, and so is removing a placeholder (a
package such as podman-docker, which conflicts with docker.io, cannot be
installed here). The guard package also Depends on every placeholder and
Conflicts with every lower version of those names, so a direct "dpkg -r runc"
or "dpkg -i containerd_1.7_arm64.deb" is refused by dpkg itself, before
anything is unpacked.

What this cannot do: root in the guest can still delete the guard, pass
--force-depends/--force-conflicts to dpkg, or unpack an engine by hand (tar,
a static docker tarball). Even then the real dockerd cannot run here, and the
managed /usr/local/bin/docker and /usr/local/bin/thothdock are read-only binds
from the app that no package can replace. ThothTerm rewrites the hook, its
configuration and the pin whenever a terminal window opens and registers the
placeholders again when the engine starts.

The Docker CLI (docker-cli, docker-ce-cli) is not restricted. ThothDock itself
is updated by the ThothTerm app, not by apt.

Check the guard with:   thothdock doctor --guard
